# Server settings cabinet: http://localhost:8080/admin
#
# FIRST CONFIGURATION (does not require a running or configured PostgreSQL):
# 1. In the run configuration, set BIKE_ADMIN_LOGIN and BIKE_ADMIN_PASSWORD (12-128 chars).
# 2. Start DemoApplication with program argument --setup (or BIKE_SETUP_MODE=true).
#    This mode always binds to 127.0.0.1 and uses an in-memory admin account/session.
# 3. Open /admin, log in, enter database URL, username/password and optional Trello credentials.
# 4. Keep BIKE_ADMIN_LOGIN and BIKE_ADMIN_PASSWORD for the first normal start:
#    AdminBootstrap will create the database ADMIN account if user_accounts is empty.
#    Save settings and use POST /api/admin/restart to start normal mode.
#    Remove --setup / BIKE_SETUP_MODE from the IDEA run configuration for future launches.
# 5. After successful normal startup, remove BIKE_ADMIN_LOGIN and BIKE_ADMIN_PASSWORD.
#
# Database must already exist; schema.sql creates tables inside it.
# Default settings file: ./config/server-settings.properties, relative to working directory.
# Set BIKE_SETTINGS_FILE to an absolute .properties path to override its location.
# Saved secrets are stored locally; file permissions are restricted to the OS account.
# SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL / USERNAME / PASSWORD and command-line datasource properties
# override the saved file. Remove those overrides to use settings from the cabinet.
# In normal mode the database pool switches after validating the new connection.
# The target database must already contain the same active ADMIN account ID.
# The target database must have the application tables and Spring Session tables.
# The administrator signs in again after a database switch.
# In --setup mode settings are saved first; POST /api/admin/restart starts the
# normal application and initializes the JPA-backed API (new JVM for JAR, new
# Spring context for IDEA). GET /api/admin/logs returns the last 500 log lines.
# Trello data is saved only; no Trello requests are made.
#
# Secrets are never returned in API responses.
# Omitted/null/empty secret field = preserve current value.
# clear...: true with no new value = explicitly remove saved secret.
# Supplying both a non-empty new value and clear...: true is rejected.
#
# Before requests below, log in using auth-api.http.
@baseUrl = http://localhost:8080

### Refresh CSRF after login; the HTTP client must retain session cookies.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/csrf

> {%
    client.global.set("csrfToken", response.body.token);
%}

### Read saved settings and presence flags (ADMIN only).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/settings

### Restart the Spring application in the current Java process (ADMIN only).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/restart
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}

### Shut down the server process (ADMIN only).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/shutdown
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}

### Save database connection while retaining all saved secrets.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}

{
  "databaseUrl": "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/bike_server",
  "databaseUsername": "postgres"
}

### Replace secrets. Define these variables in your private HTTP client environment.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}

{
  "databaseUrl": "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/bike_server",
  "databaseUsername": "postgres",
  "databasePassword": "{{databasePassword}}",
  "trelloApiKey": "{{trelloApiKey}}",
  "trelloToken": "{{trelloToken}}"
}
